<?nvs
// Attack: doc comments written the way nobody writes them. A note far longer
// than the class under it, and one line longer than any screen. Markers that
// look like the end of the note, and tag names in the middle of a sentence.
// The program has to compile and still run.
// hostile: timeout-ms 20000

/// A class whose note is a hundred times its own size.
///
/// The first thing this note does is carry every marker that means something
/// somewhere else: */ and /* and ?> and <?nvs and "an open string and 'another
/// one and a backslash \ and $name and a semicolon; and a closing brace } and
/// a <?= too. None of it ends this note. Only the end of the line does.
///
/// The body is Markdown, so it can hold anything Markdown can hold:
///
/// # A heading inside a note
/// ## And a second level
/// ### And a third
///
/// - A list
///   - inside a list
///     - inside a list
///       - inside a list
///         - inside a list
///           - inside a list
///             - and one more, deeper than anybody nests a list
///
/// | A table | with columns |
/// |---|---|
/// | and rows | that never end |
/// | and rows | that never end |
///
/// ```
/// A fenced block inside the note, holding code that would not compile:
/// class 1 { *&^%$ } ?> <?nvs */ /* and a fence that never closes: ```
/// ```
///
/// > A quotation
/// > > inside a quotation
/// > > > inside a quotation
///
/// A tag name only counts at the start of a line. In the middle of a sentence
/// it is text, so this note may say @param and @return as often as it likes,
/// and @throws and @deprecated and @internal with it. An address such as
/// name@example.com is text too, and even @see written here, in the middle of
/// a sentence, names nothing.
///
/// Text that is not plain ASCII belongs here too: Grüße, καλημέρα, こんにちは,
/// 🇦🇹 and an e with its own accent added on top: é.
///
/// One line far longer than any line a person writes by hand: this is the sort of thing that arrives when somebody pastes a whole paragraph of documentation onto one line and never breaks it up, and it keeps going, and going, and it still has to end at the newline and nowhere else, and it is still going, and going, and going, and going, and going, and going, and going, and going, and going, and going, and going, and going, and going, and going, and going, and going, and going, and going, and going, and going, and going, and going, and going, and going, and going, and going, and going, and going, and going, and going, and going, and it ends here.
///
/// @see Attack::WRAP
/// @see Attack::$seen
/// @see Attack::run
/// @see Attack::WRAP
/// @see Attack::$seen
/// @see Attack::run
/// @see Attack::WRAP
/// @see Attack::$seen
/// @see Attack::run
/// @see Attack::WRAP
/// @see Attack::$seen
/// @see Attack::run
final class Attack
{
    /// The number every round is folded back into.
    ///
    /// @see Attack::run
    public const int WRAP = 7;

    /// What the last round left behind.
    ///
    /// @see Attack::WRAP
    public int $seen;

    /// Builds a counter that has seen nothing yet.
    public function constructor()
    {
        $this->seen = 0;
    }

    /// Runs `$rounds` rounds and returns the running total.
    ///
    /// Each line of the loop feeds the next one, so none of it can be dropped.
    /// The note above this method is longer than the method, which is the
    /// whole point of it.
    ///
    /// @see Attack::WRAP
    /// @see Attack::$seen
    public function run(int $rounds): int
    {
        int $i = 0;
        while ($i < $rounds) {
            $this->seen = $this->seen + ($i % Attack::WRAP);
            $i = $i + 1;
        }
        return $this->seen;
    }
}

/// Every case of this enum carries a note of its own, and each note carries a
/// marker that means something somewhere else.
enum Marker
{
    /// A case documented with */ in its note.
    First,
    /// A case documented with /* in its note.
    Second,
    /// A case documented with ?> in its note.
    Third
}

var $attack = new Attack();
echo $attack->run(200000), "\n";
echo Marker::Third as int, "\n";
echo 'still here', "\n";
